{"name":"Green Sulphur","hex":"#9e8528","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"sulphur","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of sulfur."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative spelling of sulfur.","↑ “So long sulphur”, in Nature Chemistry‎, volume 1, number 5, August 2009, →DOI, →ISSN, page 333: “It is not a question of American or Oxford English spelling, it is a given name — and 'correcting' such a name to a different spelling is wholly inappropriate.”"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sulphur"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sulfur","blocks":[{"text":"Sulfur, also spelled as sulphur, is a chemical element; it has symbol S and atomic number 16. It is abundant, multivalent and nonmetallic. Under normal conditions, sulfur atoms form cyclic octatomic molecules with the chemical formula S8. Elemental sulfur is a bright yellow, crystalline solid at room temperature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Sulfur_-_El_Desierto_mine%2C_San_Pablo_de_Napa%2C_Daniel_Campos_Province%2C_Potos%C3%AD%2C_Bolivia.jpg/330px-Sulfur_-_El_Desierto_mine%2C_San_Pablo_de_Napa%2C_Daniel_Campos_Province%2C_Potos%C3%AD%2C_Bolivia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur","wikidata":"Q682"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Burning-sulfur.png/330px-Burning-sulfur.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Burning-sulfur.png/330px-Burning-sulfur.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"As a solid, sulfur is a characteristic lemon yellow; when burned, sulfur melts into a blood-red liquid and emits a blue flame.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABurning-sulfur.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Liquid_hydrogen_sulfide.png/330px-Liquid_hydrogen_sulfide.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Liquid_hydrogen_sulfide.png/330px-Liquid_hydrogen_sulfide.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALiquid_hydrogen_sulfide.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Sulphuric_acid_96_percent_extra_pure.jpg/500px-Sulphuric_acid_96_percent_extra_pure.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Sulphuric_acid_96_percent_extra_pure.jpg/500px-Sulphuric_acid_96_percent_extra_pure.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASulphuric_acid_96_percent_extra_pure.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Cyclooctasulfur-above-3D-balls.png/500px-Cyclooctasulfur-above-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Cyclooctasulfur-above-3D-balls.png/500px-Cyclooctasulfur-above-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The structure of the cyclooctasulfur molecule, S8","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACyclooctasulfur-above-3D-balls.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Nearly_exhausted_sulphur_vat_from_which_railroad_cars_are_loaded%2C_Freeport_Sulphur_Co.%2C_Hoskins_Mound%2C_Texas%2C_1a35438v.jpg/500px-Nearly_exhausted_sulphur_vat_from_which_railroad_cars_are_loaded%2C_Freeport_Sulphur_Co.%2C_Hoskins_Mound%2C_Texas%2C_1a35438v.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Nearly_exhausted_sulphur_vat_from_which_railroad_cars_are_loaded%2C_Freeport_Sulphur_Co.%2C_Hoskins_Mound%2C_Texas%2C_1a35438v.jpg/1280px-Nearly_exhausted_sulphur_vat_from_which_railroad_cars_are_loaded%2C_Freeport_Sulphur_Co.%2C_Hoskins_Mound%2C_Texas%2C_1a35438v.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikiped